params.body:
The network graph can now be **turned**, so a cluster can be put where the room looks at it:

- the buttons **⟲** and **⟳** turn it 15 degrees;
- **Shift + drag** (or the right mouse button) on the background turns it around the middle of the dots;
- on a tablet, **twisting two fingers** turns it around the point between them, while pinching still zooms.

The dots themselves move, not the drawing, so the names stay upright and the distances between dots stay the same. Turned positions are kept like dragged ones, and dots placed by hand turn with the rest. `rotateBy`, `turn`, `world` and `middle` in `resources/js/network-graph.js`; two buttons and one sentence in the view.

Checked in a browser against a local database with a made-up network: the buttons and shift-drag turn the picture, and the distance between two dots is unchanged (78.7 before and after). `NetworkTest` passes.
